is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: DNS service (Bind/named) on dns_server. Mechanism: Users reach the intranet by name. DNS currently returns NXDOMAIN for intranet.local, so browsers cannot resolve the hostname and never attempt an HTTP connection, which appears to users as “the page is dead.” Localization: On dns_server, the authoritative zone “local” is loaded from /etc/bind/db.local. That zone contains A records for web0.local–web3.local but no record for intranet.local. Evidence: From host_1_1_1_1, getent/host/nslookup for intranet.local returns NXDOMAIN against 10.200.0.2. Meanwhile, direct HTTP to the servers by IP works and is fast (curl to http://10.200.0.3, and with Host: intranet.local header, all web servers return valid pages). Web servers are listening on TCP/80 (netstat shows 0.0.0.0:80 LISTEN). Root cause: The intranet.local DNS record is missing from the “local” zone on dns_server. Corrective fix: Add a record for intranet.local in /etc/bind/db.local, increment the SOA serial, and reload Bind. Example options: (a) Single host: intranet IN A 10.200.0.3; or (b) simple round-robin: intranet IN A 10.200.0.3/0.4/0.5/0.6. Then run rndc reload or systemctl reload bind9. Verify with host intranet.local 10.200.0.2 and confirm that host resolution works from client hosts. Optionally add a CNAME (intranet CNAME web0.local.) instead of A if you prefer aliasing. === END ===